            <ArticleTitle>Identification and functional analysis of a novel mutation in the PAX3 gene associated with Waardenburg syndrome type I.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>362-366</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0378-1119(17)31000-4</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.gene.2017.11.035</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Waardenburg syndrome type 1 (WS1) is a rare autosomal dominant genetic disorder of neural crest cells (NCC) characterized by congenital sensorineural hearing loss, dystopia canthorum, and abnormal iris pigmentation. WS1 is due to loss-of-function mutations in paired box gene 3 (PAX3). Here, we identified a novel PAX3 mutation (c.808C&gt;G, p.R270G) in a three-generation Chinese family with WS1, and then analyzed its in vitro activities. The R270G PAX3 retained nuclear distribution and normal DNA-binding ability; however, it failed to activate MITF promoter, suggesting that haploinsufficiency may be the underlying mechanism for the mild WS1 phenotype of the study family.</AbstractText>
